      "logprobs": [],
      "text": "Yes. The right person is usually a technical SEO specialist or web developer who understands AI crawler access, robots.txt, CDNs/WAFs (especially Cloudflare), and server logs.\n\nThey should audit more than just robots.txt. For example, ChatGPT Search uses OAI-SearchBot; OpenAI says allowing it helps your pages appear in ChatGPT search results. GPTBot is separate and concerns potential model training, so you can allow search visibility without necessarily allowing training. ([developers.openai.com](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots?app=1&utm_source=openai)) Anthropic similarly has separate bots, while Google has its own controls such as Google-Extended. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawlers-fetchers/google-common-crawlers?utm_source=openai))\n\nA competent audit should check:\n- `robots.txt` and page-level `noindex`/robots directives\n- OAI-SearchBot, ChatGPT-User, Claude-related bots, Googlebot/Google-Extended, and other relevant AI/search crawlers\n- Cloudflare/Akamai/firewall/bot-protection rules that may return 403s even when robots.txt permits crawling ([help.openai.com](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001243-advertiser-guidance-for-allowing-openai-web-crawlers?utm_source=openai))\n- Whether important business, service, location, pricing, and contact pages are actually crawlable and indexable\n- XML sitemap, canonical tags, structured data/schema, and basic technical SEO\n- Server logs or direct tests to verify bots are genuinely getting through\n\nIf you give me your website URL, I can check the publicly visible crawler configuration and identify what's blocking AI/search access and what you'd need your developer to change."
